The yamlover language
description: the full spec - principles, the abstract model, concretes, pointers, queries
- >
  **yamlover** is a data language close to YAML that adds a *pointer layer* - first-class links that
  make a document a graph, not just a tree - and *concretes*: the same logical document can live in
  one file, in a directory tree, or switch language mid-stream. It is the indentation / filesystem
  surface over the shared pointer model; **json5p**, its twin, is the brace surface over the same
  model.
- >
  **The name.** *yamlover* stands for **YAML Overlay** - not "Yam lover". It means a YAML layer laid
  *over* the filesystem: a directory is a mapping, a file is a value, and one grammar reads the whole
  tree as a single document space. The file extension is `.yo` (the legacy `.yamlover` spelling is
  read forever, never written).
- >
  yamlover is *not* a superset of YAML - it is a distinct, closely-related language: close in surface,
  different in links and anchors. Because of that divergence it has its own parser, and reading is
  **concrete-aware**: a `.yaml`/`.yml` file is parsed with YAML's link semantics, a `.yo` file with
  yamlover's - each read faithfully into the one concrete-agnostic representation.
- >
  This chapter is the self-contained spec, in five parts: the [general
  principles](::language:principles) the rest follows from; the [relationship to
  YAML](::language:vs-yaml), similarity by similarity and break by break; the [abstract
  model](::language:model) - omnivorous nodes with ordered relations, the terminology and the type
  system; the [concretes](::language:concretes) - every way a node is physically stored and
  written; and [pointers and queries](::language:pointers) - paths, anchors, and the query language
  they are the singleton fragment of.
